// EXPECT: currently-rejected
// Violates: docs/SPEC.md section 2.5 (reserved words) and 7.6 (loops).
//
// KEEP THIS FILE. It is the record of why SPEC 2.5 is a language rule rather
// than a style note, and that record is still worth having now that the rule
// is enforced.
//
// The history, because the file is only interesting as a sequence:
//
//   1. It PASSED `cell check` with exit 0. A block is a valid expression, so
//      `while (total < n)` parsed as a CALL to a function named `while` and
//      the block after it was a separate expression statement, evaluated and
//      discarded. A program written in the belief that Cell has loops
//      compiled and did nothing. This was the most dangerous file in the
//      corpus.
//   2. Name resolution landed and it began failing with
//      `unknown identifier 'while'` -- rejected, but by luck rather than by
//      rule. If a function named `while` had ever been declared, it would have
//      resolved, compiled, and silently not looped again.
//   3. `while` is now a reserved word (SPEC 2.5), so this is a PARSE error:
//      `error: expected expression`, with the caret on `while`. Rejected for
//      the reason it was written to test.
//
// What is still true: Cell has no loops of any kind. Reserving the word only
// stops the silent misreading; it does not add iteration. That is union
// stage 4, and it needs an ownership rule with it, because a loop is a back
// edge and `docs/OWNERSHIP.md` section 0.3 bought its simplicity by assuming
// there were none.

pub fn count_up(copy n: Int) -> Int {
    var total = 0
    while (total < n) {
        total = total + 1
    }
    return total
}
